% Recent advancements in Bayesian Optimization (BO) for hyperparameter optimization (HPO) provide a promising foundation to address these limitations. For example, methods like fair and green hyperparameter optimization (FanG-HPO)\cite{candelieri2024fair} extend traditional BO to simultaneously optimize multiple objectives, such as accuracy, fairness, and resource efficiency, while PFNs4BO\cite{muller2023pfns4bo} and HyperBO\cite{wang2024pre} introduce pre-trained models and data-driven GP priors to enhance HPO efficiency. Additionally, freeze-thaw prior-data fitted network (FT-PFN)\cite{rakotoarison2024context} improves resource allocation in dynamic optimization settings, making it suitable for scenarios with limited computational budgets. However, these approaches primarily focus on hyperparameter optimization as an isolated process, neglecting the synergistic interactions between hyperparameter tuning and architecture search. This separation can limit the efficiency and performance of joint optimization tasks like GNN architecture search.

