In this work, we propose combining greedy rejection sampling \cite{harsha2010communication} and a linear code (which we call the inner code), yielding an efficient channel simulation scheme for small blocklengths. Our scheme can be applied to any additive noise channel with finite field vector input , i.e., $\mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{F}_{q}^{n}$, $\mathbf{Y}=\mathbf{X}+\mathbf{Z}$, where $\mathbf{Z}$ has an exchangeable distribution. We show that, as long as the inner code has a large enough distance, our scheme uses an amount of communication close to the capacity $C=\max_{p_{\mathbf{X}}}I(\mathbf{X};\mathbf{Y})$, and hence is capacity-achieving.

