\\
\>[B]{}\Conid{ℕ-Induction}\;\mathrel{=}\;{}\<[16]%
\>[16]{}(\Var p\;\mathbin{:}\;\Conid{ℕ}\;\Varid{→}\;\Conid{Set})\;(\Var{pz}\;\mathbin{:}\;\Var p\;\Con{zero})\;(\Var{ps}\;\mathbin{:}\;\Varid{∀}\;\{\mskip1.5mu \Var m\mskip1.5mu\}\;\Varid{→}\;\Var p\;\Var m\;\Varid{→}\;\Var p\;(\Con{suc}\;\Var m))\;{}\<[E]%
\\
\>[16]{}(\Var n\;\mathbin{:}\;\Conid{ℕ})\;\Varid{→}\;\Var p\;\Var n{}\<[E]%
\\[\blanklineskip]%
\>[B]{}\Varid{ind}\;\mathbin{:}\;\Conid{ℕ-Induction}{}\<[E]%
\\
\>[B]{}\Varid{ind}\;\Var p\;\Var{pz}\;\Var{ps}\;{}\<[17]%
\>[17]{}\Con{zero}\;{}\<[25]%
\>[25]{}\mathrel{=}\;\Var{pz}{}\<[E]%
\\
\>[B]{}\Varid{ind}\;\Var p\;\Var{pz}\;\Var{ps}\;({}\<[17]%
\>[17]{}\Con{suc}\;\Var n)\;{}\<[25]%
\>[25]{}\mathrel{=}\;\Var{ps}\;(\Varid{ind}\;\Var p\;\Var{pz}\;\Var{ps}\;\Var n){}\<[E]%
\ColumnHook
\end{hscode}\resethooks
The motive~\ensuremath{\Var p} is parametrically quantified, so a proof of \ensuremath{\Var p\;\Var n} has to be \ensuremath{\Var n}~applications of \ensuremath{\Var{ps}} to \ensuremath{\Var{pz}}.
There are intensionally different ways to construct that (\ensuremath{\Varid{ind}} versus a tail-recursive implementation, for example), but extensionally they're all the same.

Of course, parametricity is needed to prove that formally.
I look up \varcitet{Bernardy-proofs-for-free}{'s} translation, which (after a bit of simplification) gives the following statement:
