\section{System Overview}
\subsection{Key Ideas}
To bridge the gap between high-level programming models like Triton~\cite{Triton} and low-level programming models such as Hidet~\cite{Hidet}, Graphene~\cite{graphene}, and CUTLASS~\cite{Thakkar_CUTLASS_2023}, we propose a new tile-based programming language called Hexcute. Similar to Triton~\cite{Triton}, Hexcute is based on Python and extends Hidet~\cite{Hidet} with tile-level primitives. Hexcute \emph{exposes hardware hierarchies, such as shared memory and registers, for explicit fine-grained pipeline orchestration}. To reduce the verbosity and error-proneness of low-level programming models, Hexcute \emph{automates layout and task mapping synthesis using a type-inference-based algorithm}. Hexcute treats data distribution across threads as part of tensor types and represents them with thread-value layouts. Hexcute then constructs type relations (constraints) of layouts through algebraic operations (\emph{Section}~\ref{sec:task_mapping_constraints}), which forms a constraint programming problem. Hexcute solves the constraint system through a type-inference-based algorithm in \emph{Section}~\ref{sec:task_mapping_inference}. After inferring thread-value layouts, Hexcute reuses the constraints to select optimal instructions and generate Hidet IR (\emph{Section}~\ref{sec:instruction_selection}). The generated IR is subsequently compiled into efficient GPU code using existing compiler passes.

\subsection{Example: A Simple GEMM Kernel}
Figure \ref{fig:sample_program}~(a) illustrates a simple GEMM kernel that multiplies FP16 matrices \lstinline{a}, \lstinline{b}, and \lstinline{c} using tile-level primitives in Hexcute. In this kernel, the matrix multiplication is tiled so that each thread block is responsible for computing a $BM\times BN$ tile of the matrix \lstinline{c}.

\textbf{Lines} 3-4 define global memory tensors with user-specified layouts. Specifically, \textbf{Line} 3 interprets a sub-tensor from matrix \lstinline{a} as a three-dimensional tensor of shape \( (BM, BK, k/BK) \) with strides \( (k, 1, BK) \). Similarly, \textbf{Line} 4 reshapes a sub-tensor of \lstinline{b} into shape \( (BN, BK, k/BK) \). The decomposition converts the tensor into an iterator, which allows efficient address calculation in the following loop. \textbf{Lines} 5-7 create register tensors \lstinline{ra}, \lstinline{rb}, and \lstinline{rc}, with the compiler automatically inferring the data distribution for them. In \textbf{Line} 8, the accumulator \lstinline{rc} is initialized to zero. 

The loop in \textbf{Lines} 9-12 iterates over \( k/BK \) tiles, loading tiles of \lstinline{a} and \lstinline{b} into registers (\textbf{Lines} 10-11) and performing matrix multiply-accumulate (\lstinline{mma}) operations. After the loop, the FP32 accumulator \lstinline{rc} is cast to FP16 in \textbf{Line} 14 and rearranged via shared memory in \textbf{Line} 15 to ensure coalesced global memory writes. Here, the \lstinline{rearrange} operator is annotated with the \lstinline{auto} keyword. This hints to the compiler to determine the optimal register tensor layout to ensure coalescing the subsequent store in \textbf{Line} 16. Finally, \textbf{Lines} 15–16 store the results into matrix \lstinline{c}.

