Hexcute supports two strategies for creating shared memory tensors, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:shared_tensor}: either by explicitly declaring the shape or specifying the layout. \emph{We choose CuTe-layout~\cite{CuTe-doc} to represent the shared memory layout so that Hexcute can express the packed layout in Figure~\ref{fig:int4_packed_layout}}. In both cases, the compiler automatically resolves shared memory bank conflicts through swizzling~\cite{SwizzleInventor}. Low-precision tensors (e.g., 4-bit integers) are supported as first-class citizens (Figure~\ref{fig:shared_tensor}). Internally, register tensors use \emph{thread-value layouts} to represent distributed data across threads. Table~\ref{tab:semantic_and_syntax} summarizes the tile-level operation semantics, and Appendix~\ref{apx:lang_spec} includes a formal definition of the operator syntax.

