\subsection{Task Mapping and Layout Synthesis}
\label{sec:layout_synthesis_overview}
Hexcute provides a programming model that allows fine-grained control comparable to Hidet\cite{Hidet}, Graphene\cite{graphene}, and CUTLASS\cite{Thakkar_CUTLASS_2023}, while removing the need for error-prone specifications. Unlike the above works, Hexcute automatically infers task mappings for tile-level operations and layouts for shared/register tensors. The key insight is that \emph{the thread-value layout represents data distribution across threads and can become part of the tensor types}. Hexcute then builds type constraints (relations) on the thread-value layouts, as detailed in Section~\ref{sec:tv_layout_constraints}. The thread-value layout constraint is conceptually an equation of thread-value layouts, which can be rewritten to define infer rules, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:sample_program}~(b). For example, the constraint of the \lstinline{copy} in \textbf{Line} 9 in Figure~\ref{fig:sample_program}~(a) is formalized as: \(F\left(\text{layout}\_\text{ga}, \text{layout}\_\text{ra}\right)=0\), where \lstinline{layout}\_\lstinline{ga} and \lstinline{layout}\_\lstinline{ra} denote the thread-value layouts of \lstinline{ga[:, :, ki]} and \lstinline{ra}. 

Hexcute employs a type-inference-based algorithm to synthesize distributed register/shared memory layouts and task mappings. In the GEMM example, the compiler starts from the \lstinline{mma} operation. It selects a Tensor Core instruction \(I\) to tile the tensor \lstinline{rc}, which determines the thread-value layout \texttt{layout\_rc}, as illustrated on the left side of Figure \ref{fig:sample_program} (c). Using the constraint $G$ of the \lstinline{mma} operation, it derives the thread-value layouts for \lstinline{ra} and \lstinline{rb}. Next, applying the constraint $F$ from the \lstinline{copy} operation, it determines the layouts for \lstinline{ga[:, :, ki]} and \lstinline{gb[:, :, ki]}, as illustrated in the middle of Figure \ref{fig:sample_program} (c). Thus, the compiler incrementally propagates the thread-value layouts.

For the \lstinline{rearrange} operator, which exchanges data through shared memory, no thread-value layout constraints apply to its output \lstinline{rc1}. However, since \lstinline{rc1} is eventually stored in global memory, the optimal layout can be determined by coalescing the global memory write in \textbf{Line} 18, as illustrated on the right side of Figure \ref{fig:sample_program} (c). After inferring all layouts, the algorithm synthesizes task mappings, generates low-level IR, and compiles the IR into GPU code via existing passes. 

