\section{Thread-Value Layout Constraints}
\label{sec:task_mapping_constraints}
This section details the construction of thread-value layout constraints. We start by formalizing the behavior of memory and computing instructions. 
\subsection{Instruction Abstraction}
Modern GPU instructions such as \lstinline{ldmatrix} and \lstinline{mma} execute memory and compute tasks with coordinate thread groups. We formalize their behavior using \emph{thread-value layouts}. 

For example, in the \lstinline{ldmatrix} instruction in Figure~\ref{fig:instruction_abstraction_code}~(a), 32 threads collectively load two 8$\times$8 matrices from shared memory. Each thread is responsible for loading one row in the 16$\times$8 matrix, after which the data is redistributed across threads so that each thread holds 4 elements. This behavior can be modeled by two layouts: the layout, \lstinline{src}, which maps thread-value pairs, $(t,v)$, to logical shared memory addresses, and the layout, \lstinline{dst}, which describes the distribution of output data across threads. Similarly, thread-value layouts specify the semantics of Tensor Core instructions. For example, The instruction \lstinline{mma.sync.aligned.m16n8k16.row.col} executes small matrix multiplications with operands distributed across threads in a warp. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:instruction_abstraction_code}~(b), a thread-value layout defines the distribution of multiplicand A; the same approach applies to operands B and C. In summary, thread-value layouts fully capture the semantics of these instructions, allowing us to abstract their behavior as shown in the code examples in Figure~\ref{fig:instruction_abstraction_code}~(a) and (b).

\subsection{Thread-Value Layout Constraints}
\label{sec:tv_layout_constraints}
To automate layout and task-mapping synthesis, we first build constraints on the thread-value layout. We illustrate the derivation using a \lstinline{copy(a, b)} operation.

\subsubsection*{Composite Mapping Functions}
Let $f$ and $g$ denote the thread-value layouts of tensors \lstinline{a} and \lstinline{b}, respectively. These layouts are functions that map a thread ID $t$ and a value index $v$ to a logical coordinate in the tensor tile:
\[
f:(t, v)\mapsto(m_T, n_T),\quad g:(t, v)\mapsto(m_T, n_T)
\]
where $m_T$ and $n_T$ represent the row and column coordinates within the tensor tile.  

Suppose a memory instruction $I$ (used to implement the \lstinline{copy} operation) is specified by its own input and output layouts, denoted by $p$ and $q$, respectively. These layouts map the same thread-value pair to coordinates in the instruction tile. For instance, we might have:
\[
p:(t, v)\mapsto(m_I, m_I),\quad q:(t, v)\mapsto(m_I, n_I)
\]
where $m_I$ and $n_I$ denote the row and column coordinates within the instruction tile. 

By taking the inverse functions $p^{-1}$ and $q^{-1}$, we can convert instruction tile coordinates back to a thread-value pair. Composing these inverses with the tensor layouts gives us the composite mappings: $f\circ p^{-1}$ and $g\circ q^{-1}$, which map coordinates from the instruction tile to the corresponding positions in the input and output tensor tiles, respectively. Since the \lstinline{copy} operation transfers an identical logical region from the input tensor to the output tensor, these composite mappings must be equal: \(f\circ p^{-1} = g\circ q^{-1}\). 

\subsubsection*{An Intuitive Example of $g\circ q^{-1}$}
We consider the example of an \lstinline{ldmatrix} instruction with an output layout defined as \lstinline{q: ((4,8),(2,4)):((64,1),(32,8))}. Suppose the output tensor \lstinline{b} has a layout defined as \lstinline{g: ((4,8,2),(2,2,2)):((32,1,128),(16,8,256))}, representing two warps holding a 16$\times$32 tile. In this scenario, the mapping function $g\circ q^{-1}$ maps four $8 \times 8$ matrices produced by \lstinline{ldmatrix} to four distinct locations in the output tensor, as shown in Figure \ref{fig:mapping}.

\subsubsection*{Generalized Constraints} Figure~\ref{fig:thread_value_layout_constraints} summarizes the constraints for key operations. 
Figure~(a) formalizes the constraint for the \lstinline{copy} operation. For the \lstinline{mma} operation, we similarly construct the composite functions to bridge the instruction and the data tensors, as shown in Figure~(b). Since the tensors \lstinline{a}, \lstinline{b}, and \lstinline{c} have different coordinate spaces (i.e., \(\mathbb{Z}^{[0,M)}\times\mathbb{Z}^{[0,K)}\) for \lstinline{a}, \(\mathbb{Z}^{[0, N)}\times\mathbb{Z}^{[0, K)}\) for \lstinline{b}, and \(\mathbb{Z}^{[0,M)}\times\mathbb{Z}^{[0, N)}\) for \lstinline{c}, where \(M\), \(N\), and \(K\) denote the matrix dimensions), we define helper functions $\mu_*$ and $\eta_*$ to connect the composite mappings. For example,  $\eta_M$ maps the instruction row coordinate $m_I$ into the two-dimensional space: \(\eta_M: m_I \mapsto (m_I, 0)\), and the projection function $\mu_M$ extracts the $M$ dimension from the data tensor coordinate pair $(m_T, n_T)$: \(\mu_M: (m_T, n_T) \mapsto m_T\). A detailed discussion about the constraints for \lstinline{mma} is provided in Appendix~\ref{apx:proof_tv_layouts}. For the \lstinline{elementwise} operation, all tensors must have the same thread-value layouts, as shown in Figure~(c). Lastly, the constraint for the \lstinline{reduce} operation is provided in Figure~(d), where a projection function $\eta: \mathbb{Z}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}^{d-1}$ collapses the reduced dimension (for example, $\mu: (m, n)\mapsto m$ for a reduction along $n$ dimension).

