This section details the syntax of tile-level operations in Hexcute, which is illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:layout-syntax}. Hexcute defines memory scopes, such as \lstinline|Global|, \lstinline|Shared|, and \lstinline|Register|, to explicitly represent a tensor's memory space. In addition, Hexcute supports low-precision data types such as \lstinline{int1|2|4}, \lstinline{uint1|2|4}, and specialized floating-point formats like \lstinline{float8_e5m2}.

Building on CuTe's methodology, Hexcute employs recursively defined integer tuples to specify hierarchical shapes and layouts, which enables the construction of complex memory layouts and thread-value layouts. Layouts are expressed as colon-separated pairs of integer tuples (\lstinline|(tuple:tuple)|). Moreover, Hexcute supports primitive tensor operations in tensor programming, including data movement (\lstinline{copy}), matrix multiplication (\lstinline{mma}), type conversion (\lstinline{cast}), layout transformation via shared memory (\lstinline{rearrange}), element-wise operations, and reductions, which can be combined with arithmetic expressions to specify computations flexibly.
